window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("math-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"math-for-ai/probability-for-ai-engineers","sources":["Bishop, 2006 — Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning, chapter 1","Holtzman et al., 2020 — The Curious Case of Neural Text Degeneration","Guo et al., 2017 — On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks","Chen et al., 2021 — Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code"],"why":"- **Model outputs are distributions, not answers.** The model produces logits; softmax turns them into\n  probabilities; a sampler picks one. Treating the output as deterministic hides why the same prompt gives\n  different tool calls.\n- **Agents chain many uncertain steps.** At 95% per-step success, a 10-step run succeeds about 59.9% of\n  the time if the steps are independent. Intuition says \"95% is good\"; the product says otherwise.\n- **Detectors have base rates.** A judge that is 90% sensitive with a 10% false-positive rate, on a 5%\n  hallucination rate, is right only about 32% of the times it fires. Without Bayes you over-trust alerts.\n- **Confidence numbers drive routing.** Escalating when confidence is below 0.7 only works if 0.7 means\n  \"right 70% of the time\", which is calibration, and it must be measured, not assumed.","how":"### Distributions and softmax\n- **A discrete distribution** assigns each outcome a probability in [0, 1]; they sum to 1. The\n  vocabulary, or a set of tool names, is the outcome space.\n- **Softmax** maps logits *z* to probabilities. Temperature *T* divides the logits first:\n\n```\np_i = exp(z_i / T) / sum_j exp(z_j / T)\n```\n\n- **T below 1 sharpens** (the top token gains mass); **T above 1 flattens**; T near 0 is greedy argmax.\n  Top-p keeps the smallest set of top tokens whose probability sums to at least p and renormalizes over it.\n\n### Conditional probability and independence\n1. **Conditional:** `P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B)`, the probability of A once B is known.\n2. **Product rule:** `P(A and B) = P(A|B) · P(B)`. A sequence's probability is the product of each\n   token's probability given the tokens before it, which is exactly how an LLM scores text.\n3. **Independence:** `P(A and B) = P(A) · P(B)` only when B tells you nothing about A. Agent steps and\n   retries are often correlated (same prompt, same blind spot), so independence gives an optimistic bound.\n\n### Bayes' rule\n```\nP(H | E) = P(E | H) · P(H) / P(E),    P(E) = P(E|H)·P(H) + P(E|not H)·P(not H)\n```\n- **Prior** P(H): the base rate before evidence. **Likelihood** P(E|H): how often the evidence appears\n  when H is true. **Posterior** P(H|E): what you should believe after seeing the evidence.\n- **The base rate dominates** when it is small, because false positives from the large \"not H\" group\n  swamp true positives from the small H group.\n\n### Calibration\n- **A model is calibrated** when, among predictions made with confidence 0.8, about 80% are correct.\n- **Measure it by binning:** group predictions by stated confidence and compare each bin's mean\n  confidence with its accuracy. Expected calibration error (ECE) is the weighted gap.\n- **Fix with a held-out set.** Temperature scaling or a simple mapping fitted on labeled examples, then\n  re-check on fresh data.","example":"### Temperature, sampling, chained steps and Bayes in one script\nRuns with numpy only; every printed number below was produced by this code (numpy 2.0.2, Python 3.9).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef softmax(logits, T=1.0):\n    z = np.asarray(logits, dtype=float) / T\n    z -= z.max()                       # stability: subtract the max before exp\n    p = np.exp(z)\n    return p / p.sum()\n\n# 1. Temperature reshapes the next-token distribution (4 candidate tool names)\nlogits = [2.0, 1.0, 0.5, -1.0]\nfor T in (0.5, 1.0, 2.0):\n    print(f\"T={T}: {np.round(softmax(logits, T), 3)}\")\n\n# 2. Sampling: empirical frequencies converge to p\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\np = softmax(logits, 1.0)\ndraws = rng.choice(4, size=10_000, p=p)\nprint(\"empirical:\", np.round(np.bincount(draws, minlength=4) / 10_000, 3))\n\n# 3. Independent steps multiply: a 10-step agent with 95% per-step success\nprint(\"10-step success:\", round(0.95 ** 10, 3))\n# pass@k with per-attempt success 0.3 and independent attempts\nprint(\"pass@5:\", round(1 - (1 - 0.3) ** 5, 3))\n\n# 4. Bayes: a judge flags a hallucination. How likely is it real?\nbase, sens, fpr = 0.05, 0.90, 0.10      # P(H), P(flag|H), P(flag|not H)\np_flag = sens * base + fpr * (1 - base)\nprint(\"P(flag):\", round(p_flag, 3), \" P(H|flag):\", round(sens * base / p_flag, 3))\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nT=0.5: [0.842 0.114 0.042 0.002]\nT=1.0: [0.609 0.224 0.136 0.03 ]\nT=2.0: [0.434 0.263 0.205 0.097]\nempirical: [0.611 0.225 0.134 0.03 ]\n10-step success: 0.599\npass@5: 0.832\nP(flag): 0.14  P(H|flag): 0.321\n```\n\n- **Temperature:** at T=0.5 the top tool gets 84.2%; at T=2.0 the worst tool rises from 3% to 9.7%.\n  For tool selection that is a 3x jump in picking the wrong tool.\n- **Bayes:** of the 14% of answers the judge flags, only about a third are real hallucinations.\n\n### Worked scenario: should the agent retry or escalate?\nA support agent's refund-tool call succeeds 70% of the time on the first attempt.\n\n| Assumption | P(success within 3 attempts) | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| Attempts independent | 1 − 0.3³ = 0.973 | Retries look almost free |\n| Measured: retry succeeds 20% after a failure | 0.7 + 0.3·0.2 + 0.3·0.8·0.2 = 0.808 | Failures are sticky |\n\nThe team measured the conditional rate from traces, saw the second row, and changed policy: one retry\nwith the error message fed back, then escalate to a human.","patterns":["**Pick temperature per step type.** Use low temperature (0 to 0.3) for tool selection, argument extraction and classification, higher for drafting. Monitor tool-selection accuracy per setting on your eval set rather than trusting defaults.","**Measure conditional retry success.** Log attempt number and outcome per tool call, then compute P(success on attempt k | failed before). Set `max_retries` where that number drops below what a fallback or human costs.","**Use logprobs as a signal, then calibrate it.** Request `logprobs` where the API offers them, convert to a confidence for a label or tool, fit a threshold on labeled traces, and track a reliability diagram monthly because model updates shift it.","**Bayes-correct every alert.** For judges, guardrails and anomaly detectors, record base rate, sensitivity and false-positive rate, and report precision of flags, not just recall.","**Budget multi-step reliability.** Multiply measured per-step success rates to forecast end-to-end completion; when the product is too low, shorten the plan or add verification steps."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool choice, argument and answer is a sample from a distribution.\n  Whether a 10-step agent finishes, or a retry helps, is a probability question you can compute.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `temperature`, `top_p` and `logprobs` on model APIs, retries\n  and best-of-n in the agent loop, confidence thresholds on routers and guardrails, judge flags in evals.\n- **What you control.** The sampling settings, how many attempts you allow, which signals you combine\n  into a decision, and the threshold at which a probability becomes an action.\n- **What you can skip.** Measure theory and proofs. You need softmax, independence, the product rule,\n  Bayes' rule and the idea of calibration, all computable in a few lines of Python.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Retry and fallback policy.** Conditional success rates from traces set how many retries a tool call\n  gets before escalation.\n- **Routing on confidence.** Calibrated logprob confidence decides when a small model's answer is\n  accepted and when to escalate to a larger model or a human.\n- **Guardrail and judge triage.** Bayes' rule converts a detector's sensitivity and false-positive rate\n  into the precision reviewers will see.\n- **Sampling settings per step.** Low temperature for tool calls and extraction, higher for drafts and\n  best-of-n candidates.","mini_project":"**Temperature sweep for tool selection (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find the temperature that maximizes correct tool choice for a small agent.\n- **Steps:** write 30 user requests with a known correct tool among four; call a model at temperatures\n  0, 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5, five samples each; record the chosen tool and, where available, its logprob.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of accuracy and invalid-call rate per temperature, a reliability table\n  for logprob confidence, and a one-paragraph recommendation with the reason."},"exercise":"**Goal:** build a reliability model of one of your agent's tool calls from real or simulated traces.\n- **Inputs:** a log of at least 200 tool-call attempts with task id, attempt number, success flag and\n  any model confidence (simulate with a seeded generator if you have no traces yet).\n- **Constraints:** numpy or plain Python; no stats libraries.\n- **Deliver:** per-attempt success, the conditional P(success | earlier failures), predicted versus\n  observed completion within 3 attempts under the independence assumption, and a 10-bin reliability\n  table for the confidence column.\n- **Acceptance:** on a simulated log with independent attempts, predicted and observed completion\n  agree within about 2 points (your control); on real traces, or a simulation where each task draws its\n  own success rate, observed falls below predicted and you explain why; bin counts sum to the number of\n  attempts; one bin is checked by hand.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a coding agent uses best-of-4 sampling at temperature 0.8 and a unit-test verifier.\nSingle-sample pass rate is 40%. Product expects 1 − 0.6⁴ ≈ 87% but sees 61%. The verifier accepts 5% of\nwrong patches. Explain the gap and what you would measure and change.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- identifies that samples from one prompt are correlated, so the independence formula overstates pass@4;\n- shows why with numbers: if half the tasks pass at 0.8 and half at 0, the mean is still 0.4 but\n  pass@4 is 0.5 · (1 − 0.2⁴) ≈ 50%, because extra samples cannot rescue a task the model never solves;\n- computes the empirical pass@k per task from logged samples instead of from the single-sample rate;\n- notes verifier false accepts let wrong patches through (when all 4 samples are wrong, at least one is\n  accepted with probability 1 − 0.95⁴ ≈ 18.5%), and estimates their share with Bayes' rule;\n- proposes more diversity (different prompts or temperature), a stronger verifier, and per-task logging.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    L[\"Logits from the model\"] --> T[\"Divide by temperature T\"]\n    T --> S[\"Softmax: probabilities sum to 1\"]\n    S --> P[\"Top-p or top-k truncation\"]\n    P --> D[\"Sample one token or tool\"]\n    D --> C[\"Confidence or logprob\"]\n    C -->|\"calibrated?\"| R{\"Above threshold?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Act\"]\n    R -->|\"no\"| E[\"Retry or escalate\"]\n    E -.->|\"correlated attempts\"| D","caption":"Follow one generation step from logits to an action, and see where each probability idea enters."},"interview":[{"q":"What does temperature actually do, and what would you set it to for tool calling?","a":"- **Mechanism:** it divides the logits before softmax, so T below 1 concentrates probability on the top\n  options and T above 1 spreads it toward unlikely tokens.\n- **For tool calls:** use a low value, near 0, because you want the most likely valid call every time,\n  then confirm with tool-selection accuracy on an eval set."},{"q":"A detector flags 10% of traffic and 70% of flags are wrong. Is the detector bad?","a":"- **Not necessarily:** with a low base rate, even a sensitive detector with a modest false-positive\n  rate produces mostly false flags, which is Bayes' rule, not a bug.\n- **Next step:** measure base rate, sensitivity and false-positive rate on labeled data, then tune the\n  threshold or add a second signal to raise precision."},{"q":"Why might adding retries not improve your agent's completion rate as much as expected?","a":"- **Retries are correlated:** the same prompt, context and model repeat the same mistake, so\n  P(success | failed before) is far below the first-attempt rate.\n- **Measure and change:** compute conditional success from traces and vary something between attempts,\n  such as feeding back the error or switching model."},{"q":"How would you check whether a model's confidence scores can drive routing decisions?","a":"- **Calibration test:** on a held-out labeled set, bin predictions by confidence and compare each bin's\n  accuracy with its mean confidence, summarized as ECE.\n- **Then:** fit temperature scaling if needed, choose the threshold that meets the target precision, and\n  re-check after every model upgrade."}],"tradeoffs":["**Low vs high temperature.** Low gives repeatable tool calls but can lock in one wrong choice every time; high gives diversity for best-of-n but more malformed calls. Flips when you add a verifier.","**Independence assumption vs measured correlation.** Independence is easy to compute but overstates retry and pass@k gains; measured conditionals cost logging work but predict reality.","**Single threshold vs calibrated probability.** A fixed cutoff is simple but silently drifts with model versions; calibration needs labeled data and periodic refits."],"pitfalls":["**Assuming retries are independent.** Symptom: production completion far below `1 − (1−p)^k`. **Diagnose** by grouping traces by task and computing success on attempt 2 given attempt 1 failed. Fix: change something between attempts (error feedback, different prompt or model) or escalate sooner.","**Ignoring the base rate.** Symptom: reviewers say most judge flags are false alarms. **Diagnose** by hand-labeling 100 flagged and 100 unflagged items and computing precision with Bayes' rule. Fix: raise the threshold, add a second signal, or route flags to cheap review.","**Reading raw confidence as probability.** Symptom: answers stated at 0.9 confidence are wrong 30% of the time. **Diagnose** with a reliability diagram on a held-out set. Fix: temperature scaling or isotonic mapping, refit when the model changes.","**Changing temperature and calling it a fix.** Symptom: an eval moves after a temperature change, but only on one run. **Diagnose** by running each setting several times with different seeds. Fix: compare distributions of scores, not single runs."],"when":"- **Use when** setting sampling parameters, retry budgets, routing thresholds or alert policies.\n- **Use when** someone quotes a single accuracy for a multi-step agent; multiply out the steps.\n- **Avoid** heavy probabilistic modeling when you can measure the end-to-end rate directly on enough\n  traces; measurement beats a model of the system.\n- **Measure:** per-step success, conditional retry success, flag precision, and calibration error.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Chooses temperature, retry counts and confidence thresholds from measured probabilities instead of defaults, and logs the fields needed to compute them.","architect":"Models end-to-end agent reliability as a product of step success rates and decides where verification steps or human review raise it most cheaply.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a \"90% accurate\" detector produces mostly false alarms on rare events, using the base rate and Bayes' rule with their own numbers."},"check":{"q":"Your agent plans 8 steps, each with 97% measured success, and a guardrail flags 2% of outputs\nas unsafe with 95% sensitivity and a 1.5% false-positive rate on a 0.5% true-unsafe rate. What do you tell\nthe product owner?","a":"If steps were independent, end-to-end success is 0.97⁸ ≈ 0.784, so about one run in five fails\nsomewhere; correlated failures could make it better or worse, so confirm from traces. For the guardrail,\ntrue positives are 0.95 × 0.005 = 0.00475 and false positives are 0.015 × 0.995 ≈ 0.0149, so only\nabout 24% of flags are truly unsafe. Recommend shorter plans or verification for the agent, and a\nsecond signal or human review on flags rather than hard blocking, then measure both after the change."},"quiz":[{"q":"Raising temperature from 1.0 to 2.0 on logits [2.0, 1.0, 0.5, -1.0] does what to the least likely option?","options":["Leaves its probability unchanged because the ranking is unchanged","Raises its probability, from about 3% to about 10%","Lowers its probability toward zero","Makes all four options equally likely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dividing logits by a larger T shrinks the gaps between them, so softmax spreads mass toward unlikely options. The ranking stays the same but the tail grows, which is why high temperature hurts tool selection."},{"q":"Each of 10 agent steps succeeds independently with probability 0.95. What is the end-to-end success rate?","options":["95%","90%","About 60%","About 5%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Independent probabilities multiply: 0.95 to the 10th power is about 0.599. Small per-step error rates compound quickly in long agent runs."},{"q":"Why do most flags from a rare-event detector turn out to be false alarms even when it has high sensitivity?","options":["Sensitivity measures precision, so high sensitivity guarantees few false alarms","The non-event group is so large that its false positives outnumber the true positives","Detectors always have higher false-positive rates on rare events","Bayes' rule only applies to continuous variables"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With a 5% base rate, a 10% false-positive rate applied to 95% of items produces 9.5% false flags, versus 4.5% true flags at 90% sensitivity. Posterior precision is about 32%."}]},{"key":"math-for-ai/statistics-for-evaluation","sources":["Miller, 2024 — Adding Error Bars to Evals: A Statistical Approach to Language Model Evaluations","Efron and Tibshirani, 1993 — An Introduction to the Bootstrap","Dror et al., 2018 — The Hitchhiker's Guide to Testing Statistical Significance in Natural Language Processing","McNemar, 1947 — Note on the sampling error of the difference between correlated proportions"],"why":"- **The eval set is a sample.** Your 200 tasks stand in for all tasks users will bring. A different 200\n  would give a different score, and that spread is what a confidence interval quantifies.\n- **Small sets are noisy.** At 72% accuracy on 200 items the standard error is about 3.2 points, so the\n  95% interval is roughly ±6.2 points. A 2-point gain sits well inside that noise.\n- **Eyeballing two intervals is too strict and too loose.** Overlapping intervals do not prove \"no\n  difference\", and comparing unpaired scores throws away the fact that both models saw the same tasks.\n- **Many comparisons create false wins.** Try 20 prompt variants at the 5% level and the chance that\n  at least one looks \"significant\" by luck is about 64%.","how":"### Sampling and standard error\n- **Each task is a draw.** Pass or fail per task is a Bernoulli outcome; the eval score is the mean.\n- **Standard error (SE)** is the standard deviation of that mean across hypothetical re-samples:\n\n```\nSE = sqrt(p · (1 − p) / n)          # binary pass/fail\n95% CI ≈ p ± 1.96 · SE              # normal approximation, fine for n ≥ ~100 and p not near 0 or 1\n```\n\n- **SE shrinks with the square root of n.** At p = 0.5, n = 100 gives ±9.8 points and n = 1,000 gives\n  ±3.1 points: 10x the tasks for about 3x the precision.\n\n### Paired comparisons\n1. **Run both systems on the same tasks.** Most variation is \"this task is hard\", and pairing cancels it.\n2. **Only disagreements carry signal.** Count tasks B fixed (A failed, B passed) and tasks B broke (A\n   passed, B failed). The difference in accuracy is (fixed − broke) / n.\n3. **Test it.** McNemar's test uses only those two counts. The paired bootstrap resamples tasks, keeping\n   each task's (A, B) pair together, and reads the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the difference.\n4. **Repeated samples per task.** With temperature above 0, run each task k times and average per task\n   first, so the task, not the sample, is the unit you resample.\n\n### Significance and its limits\n- **p-value:** the probability of a difference at least this large if the true difference were zero.\n  It is not the probability the change works.\n- **Prefer intervals.** \"B − A = +2.5 points, 95% CI −2.0 to +7.0\" tells you effect size and\n  uncertainty; \"p = 0.16\" tells you less.\n- **Correct for many tries.** Hold out a fresh test split for the final pick, or apply a Bonferroni\n  threshold (0.05 divided by the number of comparisons).\n\n### Sample size\n```\nn per arm (unpaired) ≈ (z_α/2 + z_β)² · (p1(1−p1) + p2(1−p2)) / Δ²\nn (paired)           ≈ (z_α/2 + z_β)² · (d − Δ²) / Δ²       # d = share of tasks where A and B disagree\nz_α/2 = 1.96 (95%),  z_β = 0.84 (80% power)\n```\n- **Pairing is cheaper when systems mostly agree**, because d is small. Detecting 72% versus 74.5% needs\n  about 4,918 tasks per arm unpaired, but about 1,311 tasks paired when they disagree on 10.5% of tasks.","example":"### Is B's +2.5 points real? Unpaired intervals versus a paired bootstrap\nSimulated per-task results for two agent versions on the same 200 tasks (seeded, numpy 2.0.2).\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(7)\nn = 200\n# Per-item pass/fail for baseline A and candidate B on the SAME 200 eval items.\na = rng.random(n) < 0.72\nflip = rng.random(n)\nb = a.copy()\nb[(~a) & (flip < 0.30)] = True     # B fixes some of A's failures\nb[a & (flip < 0.07)] = False       # and breaks a few of A's passes\nprint(f\"A={a.mean():.3f}  B={b.mean():.3f}  diff={b.mean()-a.mean():+.3f}\")\n\n# Unpaired view: each score has its own 95% interval\nfor name, x in ((\"A\", a), (\"B\", b)):\n    se = np.sqrt(x.mean() * (1 - x.mean()) / n)\n    print(f\"{name} 95% CI: {x.mean()-1.96*se:.3f} to {x.mean()+1.96*se:.3f}\")\n\n# Paired view: only items where A and B disagree carry information\nfixed, broke = int(((~a) & b).sum()), int((a & ~b).sum())\nprint(\"fixed:\", fixed, \"broke:\", broke)\n\n# Paired bootstrap: resample ITEMS, keep each item's (A, B) pair together\nidx = rng.integers(0, n, size=(10_000, n))\ndiffs = b[idx].mean(axis=1) - a[idx].mean(axis=1)\nlo, hi = np.percentile(diffs, [2.5, 97.5])\nprint(f\"paired 95% CI for B-A: {lo:+.3f} to {hi:+.3f}\")\nprint(f\"share of resamples where B <= A: {(diffs <= 0).mean():.3f}\")\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nA=0.720  B=0.745  diff=+0.025\nA 95% CI: 0.658 to 0.782\nB 95% CI: 0.685 to 0.805\nfixed: 13 broke: 8\npaired 95% CI for B-A: -0.020 to +0.070\nshare of resamples where B <= A: 0.159\n```\n\n- **Unpaired:** two wide intervals of about ±6 points that overlap almost entirely; no conclusion.\n- **Paired:** the interval is narrower, but still includes zero. Only 21 of 200 tasks disagree, and\n  13 versus 8 is weak evidence (McNemar's statistic (13 − 8)² / 21 ≈ 1.19, well below 3.84).\n- **Decision:** not shipped on this evidence. The paired sample-size formula says about 1,311 tasks\n  would detect a true +2.5 points 80% of the time.\n\n### Worked scenario: the prompt sweep that \"won\"\nAn illustrative case: a team tried 20 system-prompt variants on a 150-task eval and shipped the best,\nwhich scored +4 points.\n\n| Step | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| 20 variants at the 5% level | about 64% chance at least one looks significant by luck |\n| Winner re-run on a fresh 300-task split | +0.7 points, paired CI −2.1 to +3.4 |\n| Root cause | selection on the same set that measured the gain |\n| New process | sweep on a dev split, confirm the single pick on a held-out split |","patterns":["**Report every score with an interval.** Have the eval harness print n, the mean and a 95% CI (or bootstrap percentiles) per metric, and refuse to render a bare percentage on dashboards.","**Gate releases on a paired lower bound.** In CI, run baseline and candidate on the same task ids and block the release when the lower bound of the paired CI for B − A is below a set margin, such as −1 point for \"no regression\".","**Size the eval before building it.** Use the sample-size formula with the smallest gain worth shipping; if the answer is 3,000 tasks, either build that many or accept that only larger gains will be detectable, and write that down.","**Repeat stochastic agents.** For temperature above 0, run each task 3 to 5 times, average per task, and bootstrap over tasks. Track per-task variance to find flaky tasks worth fixing or removing.","**Keep a held-out split.** Tune prompts and pick models on a dev split; touch the test split only to confirm the final choice, and refresh it when it leaks into prompts or few-shot examples."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Prompt, model and tool changes are judged by eval deltas of a few\n  points on a few hundred tasks. At that size, noise alone moves scores by several points.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Eval harness reports, release gates in CI, A/B dashboards,\n  judge-agreement studies, and any slide that says \"+2.5 points\".\n- **What you control.** How many tasks you run, whether you compare models on the same tasks (paired),\n  how many repeated samples per task, and which threshold a release gate uses.\n- **What you can skip.** Deriving test statistics. Know the standard error of a proportion, the paired\n  bootstrap and the sample-size formula; they cover nearly every eval decision.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Model or prompt upgrades.** Paired CIs on the golden set decide whether a new model is better,\n  equal or a regression.\n- **CI release gates.** A paired lower-bound gate blocks prompt and tool changes that reduce task\n  success beyond a margin.\n- **Customer pilots.** Sample-size math tells a customer how many labeled tickets a fair before/after\n  comparison needs.\n- **Judge validation.** Agreement statistics and repeated judging show whether an LLM judge is precise\n  enough to detect the deltas you care about.","mini_project":"**Error bars for an agent eval (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn a single-number eval into a decision-ready report.\n- **Steps:** run two prompt versions of a small tool-using agent on 100 or more shared tasks, three\n  samples each; aggregate per task; compute each mean with a CI, the paired bootstrap CI, fixed and\n  broke counts, and the sample size needed for a 3-point gain.\n- **Done when:** the report states whether the difference is real, how sure you are, and how many tasks\n  would be needed to settle it, with the code that reproduces every number."},"exercise":"**Goal:** add statistical reporting and a regression gate to an eval harness you own or a toy one.\n- **Inputs:** per-task results (task id, system, sample index, pass or score) for two systems on at least\n  100 shared tasks; simulate them with a seeded generator if needed.\n- **Constraints:** numpy or plain Python; resample tasks, not samples.\n- **Deliver:** a function returning mean, 95% CI, paired B − A CI, fixed and broke counts, and the\n  sample size needed to detect a gain you choose; a CI step that exits non-zero on regression.\n- **Acceptance:** across 200 simulated A-versus-A' runs (same per-task pass rates, fresh sampling\n  noise) the paired CI contains zero at least about 93% of the time; a planted +10-point change on 300\n  tasks is detected; and your sample-size estimate is within about 20% of a power simulation you run.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a team compares two agents with an LLM judge scoring 1 to 5. Each agent ran 120\nconversations, but the conversations differ (logged from separate weeks of traffic). Agent B averages\n3.9 versus 3.7. The judge was sampled once per conversation. They want to ship B. Advise them.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- notes the design is unpaired and confounded by week, so traffic mix could explain 0.2;\n- sizes the noise: if per-conversation scores have a standard deviation near 1 point, the SE of the\n  difference is sqrt(1/120 + 1/120) ≈ 0.13, so the 95% interval is about ±0.25 and a 0.2 gap is not established;\n- asks for per-conversation scores and a bootstrap CI for the difference in means;\n- measures judge noise by re-judging a subset and checks agreement with human labels;\n- proposes replaying the same conversations through both agents, sized with the formula.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Run A and B on the same tasks\"] --> D[\"Per-task pass or fail pairs\"]\n    D --> F[\"Count fixed and broke\"]\n    F --> B[\"Paired bootstrap or McNemar\"]\n    B --> CI[\"95% CI for B minus A\"]\n    CI -->|\"excludes 0 and gain matters\"| S[\"Ship or promote\"]\n    CI -->|\"includes 0\"| N[\"Not shown yet\"]\n    N --> Z[\"Sample-size formula\"]\n    Z -->|\"add tasks or repeats\"| R\n    S --> H[\"Confirm on held-out split\"]","caption":"Read it as the decision path from a raw eval delta to a ship decision."},"interview":[{"q":"Why are paired comparisons more powerful than comparing two independent eval scores?","a":"- **Task difficulty dominates variance:** most score variation comes from which tasks are hard, and\n  running both systems on the same tasks cancels that shared component.\n- **Only disagreements matter:** the test then rests on fixed versus broke counts, which needs far fewer\n  tasks than comparing two independent proportions."},{"q":"Your eval has 150 tasks and a candidate scores +2 points. What do you tell the team?","a":"- **Quantify the noise:** with 150 binary tasks the standard error near 75% is about 3.5 points, so a\n  2-point gain is well inside one standard error unpaired.\n- **Then:** compute the paired CI from fixed and broke counts, and if it includes zero, size the set needed\n  or treat the change as neutral."},{"q":"How would you design a CI release gate for a stochastic agent?","a":"- **Same tasks, repeats:** run baseline and candidate on identical task ids, 3 to 5 samples each, and\n  average per task before bootstrapping over tasks.\n- **Gate on a bound:** fail when the lower end of the paired CI for candidate minus baseline falls below\n  a margin such as −1 point, and log per-task flakiness."},{"q":"When would you not bother with significance testing on an eval?","a":"- **Huge effects:** when the gain is far larger than the interval width, such as +20 points on 500 tasks,\n  report the interval and decide.\n- **Tiny, cheap, reversible changes:** use an online rollout with monitoring instead of an expensive\n  offline set, while still reporting intervals there."},{"q":"How many tasks do you need to detect a 2-point improvement?","a":"- **Use the formula:** unpaired near 75% accuracy, roughly 7,000 tasks per arm at 95% confidence and\n  80% power, because the difference is small relative to binomial noise.\n- **Pair to cut it:** if the systems disagree on about 8% of tasks, a paired design needs only about\n  1,600 tasks."}],"tradeoffs":["**More tasks vs cost.** Precision grows only with the square root of n, so halving the interval needs 4x the tasks and 4x the judge or model spend. Flips when a small paired set already suffices.","**Paired vs unpaired designs.** Pairing needs both systems on identical tasks and versions, but cuts required n sharply when systems mostly agree; online A/B tests are usually unpaired by necessity.","**Bootstrap vs formula.** The bootstrap handles any metric (judge scores, cost per task, clustered data) at some compute cost; the formula is instant but assumes simple binary independent items.","**Strict gates vs speed.** A tight regression margin catches real drops but blocks harmless changes when the eval is small; loosen the margin or enlarge the set, not both silently."],"pitfalls":["**Calling a delta inside the noise a win.** Symptom: gains that vanish on the next run or in production. **Diagnose** by computing the paired CI for B − A and the number of fixed versus broke tasks. Fix: require the CI to exclude zero, or gather the sample size the formula asks for.","**Resampling the wrong unit.** Symptom: suspiciously tight intervals with repeated samples or multi-turn conversations. **Diagnose** by checking whether the bootstrap resamples individual generations instead of tasks or conversations. Fix: aggregate per task first, then resample tasks.","**Overfitting the eval set.** Symptom: each iteration improves the dev score but users notice nothing. **Diagnose** by scoring the current and a months-old version on a fresh held-out split. Fix: separate dev and test splits and rotate in new tasks.","**Ignoring judge noise.** Symptom: an LLM judge's verdicts change between runs on identical outputs. **Diagnose** by judging the same outputs twice and measuring agreement. Fix: average several judge samples, use a stronger rubric, or validate against human labels."],"when":"- **Use when** comparing prompts, models, retrieval settings or tool designs on any offline eval.\n- **Use when** setting a release gate or deciding how many tasks a new eval set needs.\n- **Avoid** significance theater on huge effects (+20 points on 500 tasks); report the interval and move on.\n- **Measure:** n, mean, paired CI of the difference, fixed and broke counts, and per-task variance.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the harness so every score carries n and a confidence interval, compares versions on the same task ids, and wires a paired regression gate into CI.","architect":"Sets eval policy: minimum set sizes per decision, held-out splits, repeat counts for stochastic agents, and the margin a release gate enforces.","fde":"Tells a customer whether a pilot's improvement is real given their sample of tickets, and how many more labeled cases would settle it."},"check":{"q":"A new retrieval setting moves your agent from 81% to 84% on 300 shared tasks: it fixes 21 tasks\nand breaks 12. Is that enough to ship, and what would you do next?","a":"The difference is (21 − 12) / 300 = 3 points. Only 33 tasks disagree, and McNemar's statistic\nis (21 − 12)² / 33 ≈ 2.45, below the 3.84 needed at the 5% level, so the evidence is suggestive, not\nconclusive. The paired 95% interval will include zero. Either run more tasks (the paired formula with\nd = 0.11 and Δ = 0.03 asks for roughly 950), or ship behind a guarded online test if the change is cheap to\nrevert, and confirm on a held-out split."},"quiz":[{"q":"An agent scores 72% on 200 tasks. Roughly what is the 95% confidence interval half-width?","options":["About 1 point","About 3 points","About 6 points","About 12 points"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The standard error is sqrt(0.72 × 0.28 / 200) ≈ 0.032, and 1.96 times that is about 0.062. So a single 200-task score is uncertain by roughly ±6 points."},{"q":"In a paired comparison of A and B on the same tasks, which tasks carry the evidence about the difference?","options":["Tasks both systems pass","Tasks both systems fail","Tasks where one system passes and the other fails","All tasks equally"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concordant tasks contribute zero to the difference. McNemar's test and the paired bootstrap's spread are driven by the counts of tasks B fixed and tasks B broke."},{"q":"You test 20 prompt variants on one eval set and ship the best. What is the main statistical risk?","options":["The confidence intervals become too wide","The winner's gain is inflated by selection, so it often shrinks on fresh tasks","Paired tests stop working after 10 comparisons","The eval set becomes too small to compute a mean"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With 20 tries at the 5% level, the chance of at least one false win is about 64%. Pick on a dev split and confirm the single winner on a held-out split."}]},{"key":"math-for-ai/linear-algebra-intuition","sources":["Strang, 2016 — Introduction to Linear Algebra, 5th edition","Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning, chapter 2","Mikolov et al., 2013 — Efficient Estimation of Word Representations in Vector Space"],"why":"- **Meaning needs a geometry.** An embedding model maps text to a point in a few hundred to a few\n  thousand dimensions so that related texts land near each other. \"Near\" must be defined, and that\n  definition is linear algebra.\n- **Ranking millions of items must be fast.** A matrix-vector product scores every stored vector against\n  a query in one operation that hardware is built for.\n- **Raw dot products mix two things.** They reward both direction (topic) and length (which, depending on the\n  model, can track text length or other quirks), so a long generic chunk can outrank a short exact one.\n- **Memory is arithmetic.** One million 1,536-dimension float32 vectors take 6.144 GB before any index\n  overhead, and that number sets your vector store bill.","how":"### Vectors and norms\n- **A vector** is a list of numbers, a point or an arrow in *d*-dimensional space.\n- **The L2 norm** is its length: `‖v‖ = sqrt(v1² + v2² + … + vd²)`.\n- **Normalizing** divides by the norm so the length becomes 1, keeping only direction.\n\n### Dot product and cosine\n```\na · b = a1·b1 + a2·b2 + … + ad·bd = ‖a‖ · ‖b‖ · cos(θ)\ncosine(a, b) = (a · b) / (‖a‖ · ‖b‖)          # in [−1, 1], ignores length\n‖a − b‖² = ‖a‖² + ‖b‖² − 2 a·b = 2 − 2·cosine   # when both are unit vectors\n```\n- **Dot product** grows with both alignment and length.\n- **Cosine** measures alignment only, because it divides out the lengths.\n- **On unit vectors** dot product equals cosine, and squared Euclidean distance ranks items in exactly\n  the reverse order of cosine, so all three metrics agree. That is why \"normalize, then use dot product\"\n  is the common recipe.\n\n### Matrices\n- **A matrix** is a stack of vectors. An index of *N* documents with dimension *d* is an `N × d` matrix.\n- **Matrix-vector product** `D @ q` gives *N* dot products, one score per document, in one call.\n- **A layer** in a neural network is `y = W x + b`: each output is a dot product of a weight row with the\n  input. Attention scores are `Q Kᵀ`, a matrix of dot products between queries and keys.\n- **Shapes must line up:** `(N × d) @ (d,)` gives `(N,)`. Most embedding bugs are shape or\n  model-version mismatches.\n\n### Dimension and memory\n| Vectors | Dimension | dtype | Raw size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1,000,000 | 1,536 | float32 (4 bytes) | 6.144 GB |\n| 1,000,000 | 1,536 | float16 (2 bytes) | 3.072 GB |\n| 1,000,000 | 1,536 | int8 (1 byte) | 1.536 GB |\n\nANN indexes such as HNSW add graph links on top of this, so treat these as lower bounds.","example":"### Dot product versus cosine on a toy index\nThree 4-dimension \"chunk\" vectors; d1 is long and generic. Tested with numpy 2.0.2.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Toy 4-d \"embeddings\". Real ones have 384-3072 dimensions; the arithmetic is identical.\nquery = np.array([0.9, 0.1, 0.0, 0.4])\ndocs = np.array([\n    [0.8, 0.2, 0.1, 0.5],    # d0: on-topic, short\n    [3.0, 2.5, 2.0, 1.0],    # d1: long, generic, large norm\n    [0.0, 0.9, 0.8, 0.1],    # d2: off-topic\n])\n\ndot = docs @ query                         # one matrix-vector product scores every doc\nnorms = np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)\ncos = dot / (norms * np.linalg.norm(query))\nprint(\"norms :\", np.round(norms, 3))\nprint(\"dot   :\", np.round(dot, 3), \"-> top\", dot.argmax())\nprint(\"cosine:\", np.round(cos, 3), \"-> top\", cos.argmax())\n\n# Normalize once at index time; then dot product == cosine and is cheaper at query time\nunit = docs / norms[:, None]\nq_unit = query / np.linalg.norm(query)\nprint(\"dot on unit vectors:\", np.round(unit @ q_unit, 3))\n\n# Euclidean distance on unit vectors ranks the same as cosine: |a-b|^2 = 2 - 2cos\nd2 = ((unit - q_unit) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\nprint(\"squared L2 on unit:\", np.round(d2, 3), \" 2-2cos:\", np.round(2 - 2 * cos, 3))\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nnorms : [0.97  4.5   1.208]\ndot   : [0.94 3.35 0.13] -> top 1\ncosine: [0.979 0.752 0.109] -> top 0\ndot on unit vectors: [0.979 0.752 0.109]\nsquared L2 on unit: [0.041 0.496 1.783]  2-2cos: [0.041 0.496 1.783]\n```\n\n- **Raw dot product** picks d1 (score 3.35) only because its norm is 4.5; cosine picks d0 (0.979).\n- **After normalizing** the dot product reproduces cosine exactly, and squared L2 equals 2 − 2·cosine.\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent's memory keeps recalling the same long note\nAn assistant stores user notes as embeddings in a vector store configured with inner product, but the\nembedding call did not normalize.\n\n| Observation | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| A 3,000-word meeting dump is retrieved for almost every query | its vector norm is the largest, so raw dot products favor it |\n| Switching the index metric to cosine fixes ranking | cosine divides out length |\n| Normalizing at write time and keeping inner product also fixes it | unit vectors make dot product equal cosine |\n| Old vectors still unnormalized after the fix | re-embed or re-normalize the stored rows |","patterns":["**Normalize once, at write time.** If the embedding model's docs recommend cosine, normalize vectors before storing (many libraries expose a flag such as `normalize_embeddings=True` in sentence-transformers `encode`, present in 2.x and later) and use inner product in the index. Monitor norms of stored vectors: they should all be 1.0.","**Match the metric to the model.** Read the embedding model card for its intended similarity; some models are trained for dot product with meaningful norms. Record the metric next to the model name in index config.","**Version embeddings with the model.** Store the model name and version with each vector and never mix versions in one index, because vectors from different models live in unrelated spaces.","**Size memory from N × d × bytes.** Compute raw size before choosing dimension or dtype, add index overhead, and consider reduced dimension or int8 storage when the eval shows recall holds.","**Batch scoring as matrix products.** For reranking or deduplication in Python, stack vectors into a matrix and use one `@` call instead of Python loops; it is often orders of magnitude faster."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Memory lookup, RAG retrieval, tool routing by description and\n  semantic caching all rank items by a vector similarity. The wrong metric silently returns the wrong\n  context to your agent.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Vector database metric settings (cosine, dot product, L2),\n  `normalize_embeddings` options in embedding libraries, embedding dimension choices, and index memory.\n- **What you control.** Whether vectors are normalized, which metric the index uses, the embedding\n  dimension and dtype, and whether query and document embeddings come from the same model version.\n- **What you can skip.** Eigenvalues, determinants and proofs. You need vectors, dot products, norms,\n  matrix-vector products and the link between cosine and Euclidean distance.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **RAG retrieval.** Metric and normalization choices decide which chunks reach the agent's context.\n- **Agent memory.** Similarity search over stored notes and past turns depends on consistent,\n  normalized embeddings from one model version.\n- **Tool routing and semantic caching.** Requests are matched to tool descriptions or cached answers by\n  cosine similarity with a threshold.\n- **Capacity planning.** Vector store size and cost follow directly from count, dimension and dtype.","mini_project":"**Build a 50-line semantic memory (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** give a chat agent a memory that retrieves relevant past notes correctly.\n- **Steps:** embed 100 notes of mixed length; store them as a normalized numpy matrix with model name\n  and version; retrieve top-3 by matrix product for 20 test questions; repeat without normalization.\n- **Done when:** you have a recall table for both versions, a printed norm check, and a short note on\n  why the normalized version wins on your data."},"exercise":"**Goal:** show, on your own data, how metric and normalization choices change what your agent retrieves.\n- **Inputs:** 200 or more text chunks of varied length from a corpus you own, 20 queries with the\n  chunk ids that should be retrieved, and any embedding model you can call.\n- **Constraints:** numpy for scoring; no vector database.\n- **Deliver:** recall@5 for raw dot product, cosine, and L2 on normalized vectors; a scatter of chunk\n  length versus vector norm; and the raw memory for your corpus at float32 and int8.\n- **Acceptance:** cosine and normalized L2 give identical rankings; you can explain any difference from\n  raw dot product using the norms you printed; one memory figure is checked by hand. If every norm is\n  already 1.0, your model normalizes for you (many embedding APIs do): record that, and repeat with a\n  model whose raw output is not unit length to see the effect.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an agent's semantic cache returns a stored answer when cosine similarity between a new\nrequest and a cached one is at least 0.92, using 1,536-dimension unit vectors.\n\nTo cut memory for 2 million entries, the team moves to a store that only supports Euclidean distance and truncates every\nstored and query vector to its first 256 components. The hit rate jumps from 18% to 41% and users get\nwrong cached answers.\n\nWhat threshold matches the old rule, why did hits jump, and what did memory drop to?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- converts the rule with ‖a − b‖² = 2 − 2·cosine: cosine ≥ 0.92 means squared L2 ≤ 0.16, or L2 ≤ 0.4,\n  and checks whether the store reports distance or squared distance;\n- explains the jump: truncated vectors are no longer unit length (norms well below 1), and dropping\n  components can only shrink distances, so far more pairs fall under a threshold set for unit vectors;\n- re-normalizes after truncating, then re-tunes the threshold on labeled same-intent and\n  different-intent request pairs, because 256-dimension similarities are distributed differently;\n- computes memory: 2,000,000 × 1,536 × 4 bytes ≈ 12.3 GB raw becomes 2,000,000 × 256 × 4 ≈ 2.05 GB,\n  before index overhead, and tracks wrong-hit rate, not just hit rate, after the change.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"User query text\"] --> EQ[\"Embed: query vector q\"]\n    Docs[\"Chunks\"] --> ED[\"Embed: matrix D, N by d\"]\n    ED --> NZ[\"Normalize rows to length 1\"]\n    EQ --> NQ[\"Normalize q\"]\n    NZ --> M[\"Scores = D times q\"]\n    NQ --> M\n    M --> K[\"Top-k by score\"]\n    K --> A[\"Context for the agent\"]\n    ED -.->|\"skip normalize\"| R[\"Long chunks dominate\"]","caption":"Trace how a query becomes a ranked list, and where normalization changes the answer."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between dot product and cosine similarity, and when are they equal?","a":"- **Dot product** multiplies matching components and sums them, so it grows with both vector length\n  and alignment.\n- **Cosine** divides the dot product by both lengths, keeping only direction; they are equal when both\n  vectors have length 1."},{"q":"Your RAG agent keeps retrieving the same few chunks for unrelated questions. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check norms and metric:** print stored vector norms and the index metric; large-norm chunks winning\n  under inner product means vectors were not normalized.\n- **Check versions and prefixes:** confirm one embedding model version and the model's required query and\n  passage formats, then measure recall@k on labeled queries after each fix."},{"q":"When would you deliberately not normalize embeddings?","a":"- **When the model uses the norm:** some embedding and recommendation models are trained for raw dot\n  product, where length encodes popularity or confidence.\n- **Decide by evidence:** follow the model card and compare recall on a labeled set with and without\n  normalization."},{"q":"How much memory do 10 million 768-dimension embeddings need, and how would you cut it?","a":"- **Raw size:** 10,000,000 × 768 × 4 bytes ≈ 30.7 GB in float32, before ANN graph or metadata overhead.\n- **Levers:** float16 or int8 storage, a smaller or truncated dimension, or product quantization, each\n  validated by recall@k on your eval set."}],"tradeoffs":["**Higher dimension vs cost.** More dimensions can capture finer distinctions but raise memory and latency linearly; flips when a smaller model or truncated dimension keeps recall on your eval.","**Cosine vs raw dot product.** Cosine is robust to length effects; dot product keeps norm information that some models use on purpose. Choose by the model card and a retrieval eval, not habit.","**Exact vs approximate search.** A full matrix product is exact but linear in N; ANN indexes trade a little recall for sublinear query time once N reaches millions."],"pitfalls":["**Unnormalized vectors under inner product.** Symptom: the same long chunks rank top for unrelated queries. **Diagnose** by printing stored vector norms and checking whether score correlates with norm. Fix: normalize and re-index, or switch the metric to cosine.","**Mixing embedding model versions.** Symptom: recall drops after a partial re-index. **Diagnose** by grouping hits by stored model version and comparing recall@k per group on a labeled query set. Fix: re-embed everything with one version, behind an alias swap.","**Query and document asymmetry ignored.** Symptom: weak retrieval with a model that expects prefixes like \"query:\" and \"passage:\". **Diagnose** by comparing recall with and without the documented prefixes on 50 labeled queries. Fix: apply the model's required input format.","**Shape mistakes that broadcast silently.** Symptom: scores have the wrong length or every score is identical. **Diagnose** by asserting shapes before the product. Fix: reshape explicitly."],"when":"- **Use when** choosing an index metric, embedding dimension or dtype, or debugging retrieval rankings.\n- **Use when** sizing a vector store or estimating the cost of brute-force scoring.\n- **Avoid** hand-rolled linear algebra for production search at millions of vectors; use the vector\n  database or an ANN library and keep this math for configuration and debugging.\n- **Measure:** stored vector norms, recall@k on labeled queries per metric, and index memory.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures vector stores with the right metric, normalizes at write time, asserts shapes, and debugs bad retrieval by inspecting norms and similarity scores.","architect":"Chooses embedding dimension, dtype and index type from memory arithmetic and recall measurements, and enforces one embedding version per index.","fde":"Explains to a customer why some documents always surface, and sizes their vector store in gigabytes from document counts and embedding dimension."},"check":{"q":"You index 5 million chunks with a 1,024-dimension model in float32 using inner product, without\nnormalizing. Users report that long policy documents appear for every question. What is wrong and\nwhat does the index cost in raw memory?","a":"Inner product on unnormalized vectors rewards vector length as well as direction, and with this\nmodel the long, dense policy chunks evidently have the largest norms (confirm by printing them), so\nthey win regardless of topic. Normalize every vector to unit\nlength and re-index, or switch the metric to cosine; on unit vectors dot product, cosine and L2 all rank the\nsame. Raw memory is 5,000,000 × 1,024 × 4 bytes ≈ 20.5 GB before index overhead, about half that in\nfloat16."},"quiz":[{"q":"For unit-length vectors, which statement is true?","options":["Dot product and cosine can rank documents differently","Dot product equals cosine, and Euclidean distance gives the reverse of the same ranking","Euclidean distance is undefined","Cosine is always zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When both lengths are 1, the cosine formula's denominator is 1, so dot product equals cosine, and squared distance equals 2 minus 2 times cosine, so smaller distance means higher cosine."},{"q":"What does the product D @ q compute when D is an N by d matrix of document embeddings?","options":["The average of all document vectors","A d by d similarity matrix","N dot products, one score per document","The norm of q"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each row of D is dotted with q, giving one similarity per document in a single vectorized operation, which is how brute-force retrieval scores a whole index."},{"q":"Roughly how much raw memory do 1 million 1,536-dimension float32 embeddings take?","options":["About 1.5 MB","About 150 MB","About 6 GB","About 60 GB"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One million times 1,536 values times 4 bytes is 6.144 billion bytes. Index structures and metadata add more on top of this raw figure."}]},{"key":"math-for-ai/calculus-intuition-for-gradients","sources":["Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning, chapters 4 and 6","Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams, 1986 — Learning representations by back-propagating errors","Kingma and Ba, 2015 — Adam: A Method for Stochastic Optimization"],"why":"- **Training is \"change weights to lower the loss\".** With billions of weights you cannot try changes\n  at random; you need the direction of steepest decrease for all weights at once, which is the gradient.\n- **Finite differences do not scale.** Nudging each weight separately needs one forward pass per weight,\n  so a 1-billion-parameter model would need a billion passes per step. The chain rule gets every\n  gradient in about one forward and one backward pass.\n- **The step size is a separate decision.** The gradient says which way; the learning rate says how far.\n  Too small wastes compute; too large overshoots and the loss explodes.\n- **Failures show up in gradient numbers.** Spikes, NaNs and flat curves all trace back to gradient size,\n  so reading them is the core debugging skill for any fine-tune.","how":"### Derivatives\n- **A derivative** `dL/dw` is the slope of the loss L as one weight w changes, holding others fixed.\n- **Sign gives direction:** positive slope means increasing w raises the loss, so move w down.\n- **Magnitude gives sensitivity:** a large slope means a small change in w moves the loss a lot.\n- **The gradient** is the vector of all partial derivatives, one per weight. It points uphill; training\n  steps the opposite way.\n\n### The chain rule\nIf `z = w·x + b`, `p = sigmoid(z)` and `L = −log p`, then:\n\n```\ndL/dw = dL/dp · dp/dz · dz/dw\n      = (−1/p) · p(1−p) · x = (p − y) · x       # for label y = 1; the same form holds for y = 0\n```\n- **Each layer contributes one local factor.** Backpropagation computes the forward values once, then\n  multiplies local derivatives from the loss backward, reusing shared pieces.\n- **Products of many factors** explain vanishing gradients (factors below 1) and exploding gradients\n  (factors above 1) in deep stacks, which residual connections, normalization and clipping counter.\n\n### Gradient descent and the learning rate\n```\nw_new = w − lr · dL/dw\n```\nOn the bowl `L(w) = (w − 3)²` the gradient is `2(w − 3)`, so the error shrinks by a factor of\n`(1 − 2·lr)` per step:\n\n| Learning rate | Factor per step | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0.1 | 0.8 | smooth, slow convergence |\n| 0.5 | 0 | lands exactly on the minimum in one step |\n| 0.9 | −0.8 | overshoots each step, oscillates, still converges |\n| 1.1 | −1.2 | overshoots more each step: diverges |\n\nReal losses are not one bowl, but the lesson holds: there is a largest stable step set by curvature,\nand going past it makes loss rise instead of fall.\n\n### What optimizers and schedules add\n- **Adam** keeps running averages of the gradient and its square, scaling each weight's step, so one\n  learning rate works across weights of very different gradient scale.\n- **Warmup** starts with a tiny learning rate while those averages settle, then ramps up.\n- **Decay** (cosine or linear) shrinks steps late in training so weights settle into a minimum.\n- **Clipping** rescales the whole gradient when its norm exceeds `max_grad_norm`, capping single bad steps.","example":"### Check a gradient by hand, then sweep learning rates\nThe analytic chain-rule gradient matches a finite-difference estimate; then four learning rates on a\nsimple bowl. Tested with numpy 2.0.2.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# One logistic \"neuron\": does this tool call succeed? z = w*x + b, p = sigmoid(z), loss = -log p\nx, y = 2.0, 1.0\ndef loss(w, b):\n    p = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(w * x + b)))\n    return -(y * np.log(p) + (1 - y) * np.log(1 - p))\n\ndef grad(w, b):\n    p = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(w * x + b)))\n    dz = p - y                       # chain rule: dL/dp * dp/dz collapses to p - y\n    return dz * x, dz                # dz/dw = x, dz/db = 1\n\nw, b, h = -0.5, 0.1, 1e-6\nnum_w = (loss(w + h, b) - loss(w - h, b)) / (2 * h)   # finite-difference check\nprint(\"analytic dL/dw:\", round(grad(w, b)[0], 6), \" numeric:\", round(num_w, 6))\n\n# Learning rate on a bowl: L(w) = (w - 3)^2, gradient 2(w - 3)\nfor lr in (0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 1.1):\n    w = 0.0\n    for step in range(20):\n        w -= lr * 2 * (w - 3)\n    print(f\"lr={lr}: w after 20 steps = {w:.3f}, loss = {(w - 3) ** 2:.3g}\")\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nanalytic dL/dw: -1.421899  numeric: -1.421899\nlr=0.1: w after 20 steps = 2.965, loss = 0.0012\nlr=0.5: w after 20 steps = 3.000, loss = 0\nlr=0.9: w after 20 steps = 2.965, loss = 0.0012\nlr=1.1: w after 20 steps = -112.013, loss = 1.32e+04\n```\n\n- **The negative gradient** (−1.42) says raising w lowers the loss, which matches intuition: the label\n  is 1 and the model currently predicts below 0.5.\n- **lr 0.1 and 0.9 end at the same place** by different paths (smooth versus oscillating); 1.1 explodes.\n\n### Worked scenario: reading a LoRA fine-tune on tool-call traces\nIllustrative logs from three runs of a small model trained on 5,000 agent traces.\n\n| Run | Learning rate | Loss curve | Grad norm | Reading |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | 2e-6 | falls very slowly, still dropping at the end | small, steady | too small: undertrained |\n| B | 2e-4 | drops fast, then flattens | steady, a few spikes clipped | healthy |\n| C | 2e-3 | drops, then spikes and becomes NaN | climbs, then inf | too large: diverged |\n\nThe team kept run B, added warmup to remove its early spikes, and confirmed with a tool-call eval rather\nthan the loss alone.","patterns":["**Run a short learning-rate sweep first.** Before a long fine-tune, train a few hundred steps at three or four learning rates spaced by about 3x to 10x, and keep the largest one whose loss falls smoothly. Monitor training and validation loss per run.","**Log grad norm every step.** Most trainers report it; plot it next to loss. A rising grad norm usually precedes a loss spike, so alert on it and on any NaN.","**Clip and warm up by default.** Set `max_grad_norm` (1.0 is a common default) and a warmup of a few percent of steps, then check how often clipping activates; constant clipping means the learning rate is too high.","**Use a schedule that decays.** Cosine or linear decay to near zero lets the model settle; compare the final validation loss with a constant-rate run on the same budget.","**Judge with a task eval, not loss.** Lower loss on traces does not guarantee better tool calls; score checkpoints on a held-out agent eval and keep the best checkpoint, not the last."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When you fine-tune a small model on agent traces (tool-call formatting,\n  routing, distillation), the only knobs that move training are the learning rate, schedule, batch size\n  and clipping, and each acts on the gradient.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `learning_rate`, `warmup_steps`, `max_grad_norm` and\n  `lr_scheduler_type` in Hugging Face `TrainingArguments` (checked against transformers 4.x), the same\n  knobs on hosted fine-tuning APIs, and the loss and grad-norm curves they log.\n- **What you control.** Step size, how it changes over time, the cap on gradient size, and which\n  parameters receive gradients at all (LoRA adapters versus full weights).\n- **What you can skip.** Hand-deriving backpropagation for a transformer and multivariable calculus\n  proofs. Autograd computes gradients; you need to know what they mean and how they fail.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Fine-tuning SLMs for tool calls.** Learning rate, warmup and clipping decide whether a small model\n  learns your tool-call format or diverges.\n- **Distillation of agent behaviour.** Reading loss and grad-norm curves tells you when the student is\n  learning versus memorizing the teacher's traces.\n- **Hosted fine-tuning.** Choosing a learning-rate multiplier and epoch count is the same gradient\n  decision with fewer knobs.\n- **Debugging training jobs.** NaNs, flat losses and spikes are diagnosed from gradient statistics.","mini_project":"**Learning-rate sweep for a tool-call formatter (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find a stable learning rate for a LoRA fine-tune of a small open model on agent traces.\n- **Steps:** prepare 1,000 short traces mapping a request to a JSON tool call; run 200 steps at three\n  learning rates spaced by about 10x; log loss and grad norm; evaluate JSON validity and tool accuracy on\n  100 held-out requests.\n- **Done when:** you have overlaid loss and grad-norm plots, an eval table per rate, and a chosen rate\n  with the evidence for it."},"exercise":"**Goal:** train a tiny classifier with hand-written gradients and reproduce the three learning-rate regimes.\n- **Inputs:** a synthetic dataset of 500 points labeling whether an agent request needs a search tool,\n  from two features you invent (for example, length and question-mark count), with a fixed seed. Keep\n  the length feature unscaled (raw characters), because logistic gradients are bounded by |x| and only\n  large inputs let a big step push the loss far above where it started.\n- **Constraints:** numpy only; implement logistic regression's forward pass, chain-rule gradients and\n  updates yourself; no autograd.\n- **Deliver:** a gradient check against finite differences, loss curves for at least four learning rates,\n  and a run with gradient clipping that survives a rate that otherwise diverges.\n- **Acceptance:** analytic and numeric gradients agree to at least 5 decimal places; one rate converges\n  smoothly, one oscillates or crawls, and one ends with loss well above its starting 0.693 without\n  clipping, and for at least one such rate, clipping the gradient norm at 1.0 keeps loss within about 2x\n  of its start. Compute loss with\n  `np.logaddexp` so large logits give big finite numbers instead of inf.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** you distill a large model's tool-calling behaviour into a 1B model. With a constant\nlearning rate the run is stable but the final checkpoint calls tools worse than a checkpoint from\nmid-training. The validation loss on held-out traces kept falling. Explain what may be happening and\nwhat you would change.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- notes validation loss and task quality can diverge: the model fits token likelihood, not tool success;\n- explains how: mean loss is dominated by many easy tokens, so the few decisive ones (tool name,\n  argument values) can get worse while the average falls; measures loss on tool-call tokens only;\n- notes held-out traces come from the teacher, so falling loss also rewards copying its style and\n  errors, and checks general skills the eval needs for forgetting;\n- confirms the gap is real with a paired comparison of the two checkpoints on the same eval tasks;\n- adds a task eval per checkpoint and keeps the best one, not the last;\n- tries learning-rate decay, fewer epochs or a lower rate, and compares on the task eval.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    X[\"Batch of agent traces\"] --> F[\"Forward pass: logits\"]\n    F --> L[\"Loss: cross-entropy\"]\n    L --> B[\"Backward: chain rule, layer by layer\"]\n    B --> G[\"Gradient for every trainable weight\"]\n    G --> C{\"Grad norm above max?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| CL[\"Clip to max norm\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| U[\"Update: w minus lr times grad\"]\n    CL --> U\n    U -->|\"next batch\"| X","caption":"Follow one training step: forward to the loss, backward through the chain rule, then the update."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the chain rule have to do with training a neural network?","a":"- **Composition:** a network is a chain of functions, and the chain rule says the derivative of the whole\n  is the product of each piece's local derivative.\n- **Backpropagation** applies it from the loss backward, reusing intermediate results, so all gradients\n  cost about one extra pass instead of one pass per weight."},{"q":"Your fine-tuning loss is flat from the first step. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check gradient flow:** count trainable parameters, run one backward pass, and confirm their gradients\n  are non-zero and not NaN.\n- **Check the loss mask and rate:** make sure label masking is not excluding every target token, and that\n  the learning rate is not effectively zero after a scheduler or multiplier bug."},{"q":"When would you choose a smaller learning rate even though training gets slower?","a":"- **When stability or retention matters:** full fine-tuning of a large model, or tuning where you must\n  keep general skills, benefits from smaller steps that change weights less.\n- **When noisy data dominates:** small batches or rare extreme examples make large steps erratic."},{"q":"How do warmup and gradient clipping each prevent divergence?","a":"- **Warmup** keeps steps tiny while Adam's running statistics are still unreliable, avoiding huge early\n  updates from noisy estimates.\n- **Clipping** caps the gradient norm on any single step, so one bad batch cannot throw the weights far\n  away; frequent clipping signals a learning rate that is too high."}],"tradeoffs":["**Large vs small learning rate.** Large steps train faster and can escape poor regions but risk divergence and forgetting; small steps are stable but may never converge within the budget.","**Aggressive clipping vs signal.** A low clip threshold prevents blow-ups but shrinks every useful step too, slowing training; it flips toward helpful when data has rare extreme examples.","**LoRA vs full fine-tuning.** Training only adapter weights uses far less memory and tolerates higher learning rates, but limits how much behaviour changes; full tuning needs smaller rates and more care with forgetting."],"pitfalls":["**Learning rate too high.** Symptom: loss spikes, then NaN, often after an initially good drop. **Diagnose** by plotting grad norm and loss per step; grad norm climbs first. Fix: lower the rate by 3x to 10x, add warmup, keep clipping, resume from the last good checkpoint.","**Learning rate too low.** Symptom: loss falls steadily but slowly and is still dropping at the end. **Diagnose** with a short sweep: a 3x larger rate falls faster without instability. Fix: raise the rate or train longer.","**Gradients not flowing.** Symptom: loss flat from step one. **Diagnose** by counting trainable parameters and checking that their gradients are non-zero after one backward pass. Fix: unfreeze the right modules, check the loss mask is not excluding every token.","**Trusting training loss alone.** Symptom: training loss keeps falling while the agent eval worsens. **Diagnose** by plotting validation loss and task metrics per checkpoint. Fix: stop early, add data, or lower the rate."],"when":"- **Use when** fine-tuning or distilling any model, including hosted fine-tuning where you set only a\n  learning-rate multiplier and epochs.\n- **Use when** a training run misbehaves and you must decide between data, rate and code bugs.\n- **Avoid** gradient-level tuning when prompting or retrieval already meets the target; fine-tuning\n  adds a training pipeline to maintain.\n- **Measure:** training and validation loss, grad norm, clip rate, and the task eval per checkpoint.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets learning rate, warmup, schedule and clipping for fine-tunes on agent traces, and debugs diverging or flat runs from loss and grad-norm plots.","architect":"Decides whether a behaviour should be fixed by fine-tuning at all, and what training budget, sweep size and evaluation gates a fine-tuning pipeline needs.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their fine-tune failed (rate too high, no validation split) and what to change, using the loss curve they already have."},"check":{"q":"A LoRA fine-tune on 8,000 tool-call traces shows training loss falling for 300 steps, then\nspiking and going to NaN at step 420. Grad norm rose from about 1 to over 50 before the spike. What\nhappened and what do you change?","a":"The updates became too large for the loss surface: grad norm climbing before the spike is the\nclassic sign of a learning rate past the stable limit, possibly triggered by a few extreme examples. Resume\nfrom the last good checkpoint, cut the learning rate by about 3x to 10x, add warmup if missing, keep\ngradient clipping on (for example `max_grad_norm` 1.0) and check how often it fires. Also inspect the\nbatches around step 420 for malformed or extremely long traces, and judge the rerun with a held-out agent\neval rather than training loss alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"On the loss L(w) = (w − 3)², which learning rate makes gradient descent diverge?","options":["0.1","0.5","0.9","1.1"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each step multiplies the error by (1 − 2·lr). For 1.1 the factor is −1.2, so the error grows every step and flips sign; rates up to 1.0 keep the factor's magnitude at most 1."},{"q":"Why does backpropagation compute all gradients far more cheaply than finite differences?","options":["It skips the forward pass","The chain rule reuses intermediate results, so one backward pass yields every gradient","It only computes gradients for the last layer","It approximates gradients with random samples"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Finite differences need a separate forward pass per weight. Backpropagation multiplies local derivatives backward once, sharing the work across all weights."},{"q":"Grad norm climbs steadily, then loss spikes to NaN. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The learning rate is too small","The validation set is too small","The learning rate is too large for the loss surface","The model has too few layers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Steps larger than the stable limit overshoot, raising loss and gradients each step until values overflow. Lower the rate, add warmup and keep clipping."}]},{"key":"math-for-ai/information-theory-basics","sources":["Shannon, 1948 — A Mathematical Theory of Communication","Cover and Thomas, 2006 — Elements of Information Theory, 2nd edition","Hinton, Vinyals and Dean, 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback"],"why":"- **A loss must reward the right probabilities.** Accuracy only checks the top token; cross-entropy\n  scores how much probability the model gave the correct token, so it improves smoothly and has useful\n  gradients.\n- **Loss numbers need an interpretation.** A mean loss of 2.0 nats per token means perplexity\n  exp(2.0) ≈ 7.39: the model is as unsure as a uniform choice among about 7 tokens.\n- **Tuning needs a leash.** Optimizing a reward model alone drifts into degenerate text that games\n  the reward; a KL penalty against the original model keeps outputs close to fluent language.\n- **Distillation needs a target richer than one label.** A teacher's full distribution says which wrong\n  answers are nearly right; matching it with KL transfers that structure to a student.","how":"### Surprise and entropy\n```\nsurprise(x)   = −log p(x)                      # rare events carry more information\nH(p)          = −Σ p(x) · log p(x)             # expected surprise: uncertainty of p\n```\n- **Units:** natural log gives nats, log base 2 gives bits; divide nats by ln 2 ≈ 0.693 to get bits.\n  Training frameworks report nats.\n- **Range:** entropy is 0 when one outcome is certain and largest, log N, when N outcomes are equally\n  likely. A uniform 50,000-token vocabulary has entropy ln 50,000 ≈ 10.82 nats.\n\n### Cross-entropy\n```\nH(p, q) = −Σ p(x) · log q(x)                   # cost of coding p's events using q\n```\n- **With a one-hot target** (the actual next token), cross-entropy is just `−log q(correct token)`.\n  That is the standard LLM training loss, averaged over tokens.\n- **It is never below H(p).** The gap is exactly the KL divergence.\n\n### KL divergence\n```\nKL(p || q) = Σ p(x) · log(p(x) / q(x)) = H(p, q) − H(p)    ≥ 0, zero only when p = q\n```\n- **Not symmetric:** KL(p‖q) and KL(q‖p) differ. Forward KL (truth first) punishes q for missing\n  outcomes p cares about; reverse KL punishes q for putting mass where p has little.\n- **In RLHF:** the objective is reward minus β · KL(policy ‖ reference), estimated on sampled tokens,\n  so the policy improves reward while staying near the starting model. DPO bakes the same constraint\n  into its loss through β.\n- **In distillation:** the student minimizes KL(teacher ‖ student) on temperature-softened\n  distributions, often mixed with ordinary cross-entropy on true labels.\n\n### Perplexity\n```\nperplexity = exp(mean per-token cross-entropy in nats)\n```\n- **Lower is better**, and it only compares models that use the same tokenizer and the same text,\n  because token boundaries change the per-token average.","example":"### Entropy, cross-entropy, KL and perplexity on real numbers\nA teacher and a student distribution over four next tokens, then a five-token loss. Tested with numpy 2.0.2.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef entropy(p):          # in nats; divide by log(2) for bits\n    p = np.asarray(p, float); p = p[p > 0]\n    return -(p * np.log(p)).sum()\n\ndef cross_entropy(p, q): # expected -log q under the true distribution p\n    p, q = np.asarray(p, float), np.asarray(q, float)\n    return -(p * np.log(q)).sum()\n\ndef kl(p, q):            # extra nats paid for using q when the truth is p\n    return cross_entropy(p, q) - entropy(p)\n\n# Next-token distributions over 4 tokens\nteacher = [0.70, 0.20, 0.05, 0.05]\nstudent = [0.40, 0.40, 0.10, 0.10]\nprint(f\"H(teacher)        = {entropy(teacher):.3f} nats = {entropy(teacher)/np.log(2):.3f} bits\")\nprint(f\"CE(teacher, stud) = {cross_entropy(teacher, student):.3f}\")\nprint(f\"KL(teacher||stud) = {kl(teacher, student):.3f}\")\nprint(f\"KL(stud||teacher) = {kl(student, teacher):.3f}   (not symmetric)\")\n\n# Training loss on hard labels is CE with a one-hot target = -log p(correct token)\np_correct = np.array([0.9, 0.5, 0.1, 0.6, 0.8])   # model's prob of each true next token\nnll = -np.log(p_correct)\nprint(\"per-token loss:\", np.round(nll, 3))\nprint(f\"mean loss = {nll.mean():.3f}, perplexity = exp(mean) = {np.exp(nll.mean()):.3f}\")\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nH(teacher)        = 0.871 nats = 1.257 bits\nCE(teacher, stud) = 1.055\nKL(teacher||stud) = 0.184\nKL(stud||teacher) = 0.192   (not symmetric)\nper-token loss: [0.105 0.693 2.303 0.511 0.223]\nmean loss = 0.767, perplexity = exp(mean) = 2.153\n```\n\n- **CE = H + KL:** 1.055 = 0.871 + 0.184; the student pays 0.184 extra nats per token.\n- **One bad token dominates:** the token given 0.1 probability costs 2.303 nats, more than the other\n  four combined, which is why loss is sensitive to confidently wrong predictions.\n\n### Worked scenario: a distilled routing agent\nA team distills a large model's tool-routing decisions (8 tools) into a small classifier head.\n\n| Training target | Held-out routing accuracy | What the student learns |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hard labels, cross-entropy | baseline | only the teacher's top choice |\n| Teacher soft probabilities, KL at T = 2 | compared on the same split | which tools are near-substitutes |\n| Mixed: 0.5 · CE on labels + 0.5 · KL | chosen if it wins on the eval | both |\n\nThe point is the decision procedure, not a promised gain: train each target, compare on the same\nheld-out routing set with the paired statistics from the evaluation lesson, and keep the winner.","patterns":["**Track loss in nats and perplexity together.** Log mean per-token training and validation loss, convert to perplexity for reports, and only compare runs that share a tokenizer and eval text.","**Use token logprobs as an uncertainty signal.** Request `logprobs` where the API supports them, compute the mean negative logprob of a generated tool call or answer, and route high-surprise outputs to verification. Calibrate the threshold on labeled traces first.","**Watch the KL in preference tuning.** Libraries for RLHF-style training log the KL between policy and reference; a KL that climbs fast with rising reward usually means reward hacking. Tune β and inspect samples at each checkpoint.","**Distill with soft targets when you can get them.** If the teacher exposes logprobs (self-hosted or API top-k logprobs), add a KL term on softened distributions; otherwise fall back to hard-label fine-tuning on teacher outputs.","**Use entropy of a choice as a gate.** For a classifier or router, high entropy over options means the model is unsure; send those cases to a larger model or a human."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Fine-tuning an SLM on agent traces minimizes cross-entropy; distilling\n  a large agent into a small one minimizes a KL; RLHF-style tuning keeps a KL leash on the policy.\n  Reading those numbers tells you whether training is working.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Training loss in any trainer, `logprobs` from model APIs,\n  perplexity reports on model cards, the KL coefficient in preference-tuning libraries, and token-level\n  uncertainty signals.\n- **What you control.** Which distribution you train toward (hard labels or a teacher's soft\n  probabilities), the KL penalty strength, and whether you use entropy or logprobs as a routing signal.\n- **What you can skip.** Coding theory and channel capacity. You need the four formulas below, the\n  units (nats or bits), and the asymmetry of KL.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Fine-tuning SLMs for agents.** Cross-entropy on traces is the training signal, and validation loss\n  plus perplexity track fit.\n- **Distilling agent behaviour.** KL on a teacher's soft distribution transfers routing and tool-choice\n  structure to a smaller model.\n- **Preference tuning.** The KL penalty keeps a tuned agent close to its reference while improving\n  helpfulness.\n- **Uncertainty routing.** Token logprobs and choice entropy flag low-confidence tool calls for\n  verification or escalation.","mini_project":"**Logprob-based escalation for a routing agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** route uncertain tool choices to a stronger model and measure the gain.\n- **Steps:** build a router over five tools with a small model that returns logprobs; compute entropy\n  over the tool options per request; send the top 20% most uncertain to a larger model; evaluate on 100\n  labeled requests.\n- **Done when:** you have accuracy and cost for small-only, large-only and escalation setups, plus the\n  entropy threshold and how you chose it."},"exercise":"**Goal:** use logprobs to build an uncertainty signal for an agent's tool calls and test whether it works.\n- **Inputs:** 100 or more agent requests with known correct tool and arguments, and a model or API that\n  returns token logprobs.\n- **Constraints:** compute entropy, mean negative logprob and perplexity yourself from the returned\n  values; state units. APIs return only the top few alternatives per token, so call your entropy a\n  truncated estimate.\n- **Deliver:** per-call mean negative logprob and perplexity, accuracy by quintile of that signal, and a\n  threshold that routes the least confident 20% of calls to review.\n- **Acceptance:** you report the accuracy gap between the least and most confident quintiles with a\n  95% interval and say whether the signal is usable at your sample size; one perplexity value is\n  recomputed by hand from its logprobs; units are labeled everywhere.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** you must pick a base model to fine-tune for a support agent. Both vendors scored the same\n10,000-character support-chat validation text.\n\nModel A's tokenizer splits it into 2,500 tokens and A\nreports perplexity 6.0; model B's splits it into 3,200 tokens and B reports perplexity 4.5. A teammate\nsays B fits your domain better. Is that right, and how would you decide?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- turns each perplexity back into total nats: tokens × ln(perplexity), so A ≈ 2,500 × 1.792 ≈ 4,479\n  and B ≈ 3,200 × 1.504 ≈ 4,813 nats for the same text;\n- divides by the shared unit, characters: A ≈ 0.448 nats ≈ 0.646 bits per character, B ≈ 0.481 nats\n  ≈ 0.694 bits per character, so A assigns the text more probability despite its higher perplexity;\n- explains that B's lower number comes from cutting the text into more, easier tokens, which lowers\n  the per-token average without improving fit;\n- still does not choose on fit alone: compares both on a tool-call and task eval, because likelihood\n  of support text is not task success.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Model next-token distribution q\"] --> CE[\"Cross-entropy vs true token\"]\n    CE --> TL[\"Training loss in nats\"]\n    TL --> PPL[\"Perplexity = exp of mean loss\"]\n    T[\"Teacher distribution p\"] --> KD[\"KL teacher to student\"]\n    M --> KD\n    KD --> DS[\"Distillation loss\"]\n    R[\"Reference model\"] --> KR[\"KL policy to reference\"]\n    M --> KR\n    KR --> RL[\"RLHF: reward minus beta times KL\"]","caption":"See how one quantity, the probability the model assigns, feeds loss, perplexity and the two KL uses."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do language models train on cross-entropy rather than accuracy?","a":"- **Smooth and informative:** cross-entropy rewards raising the correct token's probability even when it\n  is not yet the top choice, so every example gives a useful gradient.\n- **Proper scoring:** it is minimized by predicting the true distribution, which is what sampling needs;\n  accuracy ignores how much probability went where."},{"q":"What is the KL term in RLHF for, and what happens if you remove it?","a":"- **A leash:** it penalizes the policy for drifting from the reference model, so the model keeps fluent,\n  general behaviour while improving the reward.\n- **Without it:** the policy exploits reward-model blind spots, producing high-reward but degenerate or\n  repetitive text, and can lose capabilities."},{"q":"Your distilled student matches the teacher's top choice but generalizes poorly. What would you try?","a":"- **Soft targets:** train on the teacher's softened full distribution with a KL term, so the student\n  learns which alternatives are close, not just the winner.\n- **Check the mix and data:** combine with cross-entropy on true labels, widen trace coverage, and\n  compare variants on a held-out agent eval."},{"q":"When is perplexity a misleading metric?","a":"- **Across tokenizers or texts:** per-token averages change with token boundaries, so numbers from\n  different tokenizers or eval sets are not comparable.\n- **For task quality:** lower perplexity on traces does not guarantee better tool calls or answers, so\n  agents need task evals."}],"tradeoffs":["**Forward vs reverse KL.** Forward KL makes the student cover every outcome the teacher likes, which can blur; reverse KL makes it commit to one mode, which can drop rare but valid behaviours.","**Strong vs weak KL penalty.** A large β keeps the tuned model safe and fluent but limits gains; a small β allows larger behaviour change at the risk of reward hacking and lost capabilities.","**Perplexity vs task metrics.** Perplexity is cheap and smooth but measures fit to text, not task success; an agent can get lower perplexity and worse tool accuracy."],"pitfalls":["**Comparing perplexity across tokenizers.** Symptom: a model \"wins\" on perplexity but loses every task eval. **Diagnose** by checking the tokenizers and the eval text; different token counts change the average. Fix: compare bits per byte or character, or use task metrics.","**Mixing nats and bits.** Symptom: a loss target that seems impossible or trivially met. **Diagnose** by checking the log base in the code that reports it. Fix: convert with ln 2 and state units.","**Reward climbs, quality falls in preference tuning.** Symptom: reward model score rises while human ratings drop. **Diagnose** by plotting KL to the reference and reading samples; a fast-growing KL signals reward hacking. Fix: raise β, stop earlier, or improve the reward model.","**Logprob confidence used uncalibrated.** Symptom: high-logprob tool calls that are still wrong. **Diagnose** by binning calls by mean logprob and measuring accuracy per bin. Fix: fit the threshold on labeled data and refit after model changes."],"when":"- **Use when** reading training curves, comparing checkpoints, or setting up distillation or preference tuning.\n- **Use when** building uncertainty-based routing from logprobs or choice entropy.\n- **Avoid** perplexity as the decision metric for agent quality; use task evals for shipping decisions.\n- **Measure:** validation loss, perplexity on fixed text, KL to reference during tuning, and\n  calibration of logprob-based signals.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Reads loss and perplexity correctly, uses logprobs for uncertainty routing, and sets up distillation losses with soft targets when the teacher exposes them.","architect":"Chooses between hard-label fine-tuning, distillation and preference tuning, and decides which information-theoretic signals become production metrics.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a lower-perplexity model is not automatically better for their agent, and what task metric to compare instead."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tune on agent traces reports validation loss falling from 1.20 to 0.90. A teammate says\n\"perplexity dropped by a quarter\". Is that right, and does it mean the agent got better?","a":"Perplexity is exp(loss), so it went from exp(1.20) ≈ 3.32 to exp(0.90) ≈ 2.46, a drop of\nabout 26%; the loss fell 25%, so the teammate's number is close by coincidence, not by rule. It\nmeans the model assigns more probability to the trace tokens, which is fit to the data, not task success.\nCheck with a held-out agent eval (tool accuracy, task completion) before claiming improvement, and make\nsure both numbers use the same tokenizer and validation text."},"quiz":[{"q":"A model's mean per-token cross-entropy on a validation set is 2.0 nats. What is its perplexity?","options":["2.0","4.0","About 7.39","About 0.69"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Perplexity is exp of the mean loss in nats, and exp(2.0) is about 7.39. The model is as uncertain as a uniform choice among roughly 7 tokens."},{"q":"Which identity links entropy, cross-entropy and KL divergence?","options":["Cross-entropy H(p, q) equals entropy H(p) plus KL(p‖q)","KL(p‖q) equals H(p) plus H(p, q)","Entropy equals cross-entropy times KL","KL is the same as cross-entropy when p is uniform"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The extra cost of using q to describe events from p is exactly the KL divergence. Because KL is never negative, cross-entropy is never below the entropy of p."},{"q":"Why is KL(p‖q) usually different from KL(q‖p)?","options":["Because of floating-point rounding","Each direction weights the log ratio by a different distribution, so they penalize different mismatches","They are always equal; the order does not matter","Only KL(q‖p) can be negative"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Forward KL averages over p and punishes q for missing what p covers. Reverse KL averages over q and punishes mass where p is small, which is why distillation and RLHF choose directions deliberately."}]},{"key":"math-for-ai/numerical-precision","sources":["Goldberg, 1991 — What Every Computer Scientist Should Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic","IEEE 754-2008 — IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic","Micikevicius et al., 2018 — Mixed Precision Training","Kalamkar et al., 2019 — A Study of BFLOAT16 for Deep Learning Training","Dettmers et al., 2022 — LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale"],"why":"- **Memory sets what you can serve.** A 7B-parameter model needs about 28 GB in fp32, 14 GB in bf16,\n  7 GB in int8 and 3.5 GB in 4-bit, so dtype decides whether it fits a given GPU or laptop.\n- **Fewer bits are faster.** Decode is bound by memory bandwidth, so halving bytes per weight roughly\n  halves the bytes read per token, and tensor cores run low-precision math at higher throughput.\n- **But fewer bits break things.** fp16 overflows above 65,504, so `exp(12)` (about 162,755) becomes\n  infinity and a softmax becomes NaN. Small updates can vanish when added to large numbers.\n- **Floating point is not real arithmetic.** In float64, `(0.1 + 0.2) + 0.3` is 0.6000000000000001\n  but `0.1 + (0.2 + 0.3)` is 0.6, so summation order changes results and replays drift.","how":"### Floating point in one line\n```\nvalue = sign × 1.mantissa × 2^(exponent − bias)\n```\n- **Exponent bits set range** (how large or small a number can be).\n- **Mantissa bits set precision** (how many significant digits). Machine epsilon is the gap between 1\n  and the next representable number: 2 to the power of minus the mantissa bits.\n\n### The formats you will meet\n| Format | Exponent / mantissa bits | Max value | Epsilon | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fp32 | 8 / 23 | ~3.4e38 | ~1.19e-7 | master weights, optimizer state, accumulators |\n| fp16 | 5 / 10 | 65,504 | ~0.000977 | inference, older mixed-precision training |\n| bf16 | 8 / 7 | ~3.39e38 | 0.0078125 | modern training and inference |\n| fp8 E4M3 / E5M2 | 4 / 3, 5 / 2 | 448 / 57,344 | coarse | KV caches, newer training and serving |\n| int8, int4 | integers plus a scale | scale × 127, scale × 7 | set by scale | weight quantization |\n\n- **bf16 keeps fp32's range with fewer digits**, so it rarely overflows but rounds coarsely: 1.001 becomes 1.0.\n- **fp16 has more digits but tiny range:** its smallest normal value is about 6.1e-5, so small gradients\n  underflow; fp16 training therefore uses loss scaling, while bf16 usually does not need it.\n\n### Three failure mechanisms\n1. **Overflow:** a value beyond the max becomes inf; `inf − inf` or `inf / inf` gives NaN, which spreads\n   through every later operation. In fp16, `exp(x)` overflows for x above about 11.09.\n2. **Underflow:** values below the smallest representable become zero (gradients disappear).\n3. **Swamping:** adding a small number to a large one loses it. In fp16, 2048 + 1 rounds back to 2048,\n   because the gap between representable numbers there is 2.\n\n### The fixes frameworks already apply\n- **Max subtraction in softmax:** `exp(z − max z)` keeps every exponent at most 0, which cannot overflow.\n- **Higher-precision accumulation:** matrix multiplies in fp16 or bf16 accumulate in fp32.\n- **fp32 master weights:** mixed-precision training keeps an fp32 copy so tiny updates are not swamped.\n\n### Integer quantization\n```\nscale = max(|w|) / 127          # symmetric int8, per tensor, channel or group\nq     = round(w / scale)         # stored as int8\nw_hat = q × scale                # dequantized at compute time\n```\n- **One outlier sets the scale** for its whole group, so small weights get few distinct levels.\n  Per-channel or per-group scales (groups of 64 or 128 weights are common) and outlier handling, as in\n  LLM.int8(), limit the damage.","example":"### Overflow, swamping, bf16 rounding and int8 outliers\nnumpy has no native bf16, so the script emulates it by rounding fp32 to its top 16 bits. Tested with\nnumpy 2.0.2.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nnp.seterr(over=\"ignore\", invalid=\"ignore\")   # we want to SEE inf and nan here\n\ndef to_bf16(x):\n    \"\"\"Round float32 to bfloat16 (keep the top 16 bits, round to nearest even), back as float32.\"\"\"\n    b = np.asarray(x, np.float32).view(np.uint32)\n    b = (b + 0x7FFF + ((b >> 16) & 1)) & 0xFFFF0000\n    return b.view(np.float32)\n\nfp16 = np.finfo(np.float16)\nprint(\"fp16 max:\", float(fp16.max), \" eps:\", fp16.eps, \" smallest normal:\", fp16.tiny)\nprint(\"bf16 max:\", (2 - 2.0 ** -7) * 2.0 ** 127, \" eps:\", 2.0 ** -7)\n\n# 1. Overflow: logits of 12 are ordinary, but exp(12) does not fit in fp16\nlogits = np.array([12.0, 10.0, 3.0], np.float16)\nnaive = np.exp(logits) / np.exp(logits).sum()\nstable = np.exp(logits - logits.max()) / np.exp(logits - logits.max()).sum()\nprint(\"naive fp16 softmax:\", naive, \" stable:\", stable)\n\n# 2. Swamping: small updates vanish when added to a large fp16 accumulator\nacc = np.float16(2048)\nfor _ in range(100):\n    acc = np.float16(acc + np.float16(1))\nprint(\"2048 + 1 (x100) in fp16 =\", acc, \" in fp32 =\", np.float32(2048) + 100)\n\n# 3. bf16 keeps range, loses digits\nprint(\"bf16(1.001) =\", to_bf16(1.001), \" bf16(70000) =\", to_bf16(70000.0),\n      \" fp16(70000) =\", np.float16(np.float32(70000.0)))\n\n# 4. int8 symmetric quantization of a weight row with one outlier\nw = np.array([0.02, -0.05, 0.03, 0.01, -0.02, 2.5], np.float32)\nscale = np.abs(w).max() / 127\nq = np.clip(np.round(w / scale), -127, 127).astype(np.int8)\nprint(\"int8:\", q, \" dequant:\", np.round(q * scale, 4), \" scale:\", round(float(scale), 5))\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nfp16 max: 65504.0  eps: 0.000977  smallest normal: 6.104e-05\nbf16 max: 3.3895313892515355e+38  eps: 0.0078125\nnaive fp16 softmax: [nan  0.  0.]  stable: [8.8037e-01 1.1920e-01 1.0866e-04]\n2048 + 1 (x100) in fp16 = 2048.0  in fp32 = 2148.0\nbf16(1.001) = 1.0  bf16(70000) = 70144.0  fp16(70000) = inf\nint8: [  1  -3   2   1  -1 127]  dequant: [ 0.0197 -0.0591  0.0394  0.0197 -0.0197  2.5   ]  scale: 0.01969\n```\n\n- **Overflow:** the naive softmax returns NaN; subtracting the max gives valid probabilities.\n- **Swamping:** a hundred additions of 1 change nothing in fp16, while fp32 reaches 2148.\n- **Range versus digits:** bf16 stores 70,000 as 70,144 (coarse but finite); fp16 gives inf.\n- **Outlier:** the 2.5 weight forces a scale of 0.0197, so −0.05 becomes −0.0591 (18% error) and 0.03\n  becomes 0.0394 (31%). Without the outlier the scale would be about 0.00039, 50x finer.\n\n### Worked scenario: a local agent emits garbage after a dtype change\nAn illustrative case: a team moved a bf16-trained 7B model to an older GPU without bf16 support and\nloaded it in fp16 to save memory.\n\n| Observation | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Some prompts return empty or repeated tokens | activations above 65,504 overflow to inf, then NaN | load in fp32 on that GPU, or use a GPU with bf16 |\n| Short prompts look fine | large activations appear only on some inputs | test long and tool-heavy prompts |\n| 4-bit checkpoint works but tool JSON breaks more often | quantization error shifts close logits | compare tool-call validity versus bf16 on an eval |","patterns":["**Serve in bf16 by default on hardware that supports it.** Set `torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16` (or `dtype=\"bfloat16\"` in vLLM) for models trained in bf16, and avoid casting them to fp16. Monitor for NaN logits and empty generations.","**Quantize with an agent eval as the gate.** Before shipping a 4-bit or 8-bit checkpoint, run the same tool-calling and task eval on both precisions and compare with a paired test; JSON validity and argument accuracy often degrade before general chat quality.","**Quantize the KV cache separately.** An FP8 KV cache (`kv_cache_dtype=\"fp8\"` in vLLM, option name as of 0.6.x) roughly doubles resident sequences versus fp16 at small quality cost; evaluate long-context agent runs, where errors accumulate.","**Keep sensitive math in fp32.** Softmax, layer norm, loss computation and accumulations stay in fp32 inside mixed-precision code; when writing custom scoring or reranking code, do the same.","**Make replay tests tolerant.** Batch size, kernel choice and summation order change low-order bits, so even greedy decoding can diverge on near-ties. Compare agent replays on outcomes and tool sequences, not exact text."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Running an agent on a self-hosted or local model means choosing a\n  dtype or quantization level. That choice sets GPU memory, speed, and whether tool calls stay correct.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `torch_dtype` in Hugging Face loaders (renamed `dtype` in\n  transformers 4.56+, old name still accepted with a warning), `dtype` and `kv_cache_dtype` in vLLM, quantized checkpoint formats (GPTQ, AWQ, GGUF), mixed-precision training\n  flags, and NaN errors in logs.\n- **What you control.** The weight dtype, the KV-cache dtype, the quantization method and group size,\n  and whether softmax and accumulations run in higher precision.\n- **What you can skip.** Bit-level IEEE rounding modes and hardware microarchitecture. Know the table\n  of formats, the overflow and swamping failures, and how int8 scales work.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local and edge agents.** Choosing 4-bit or 8-bit checkpoints decides whether an agent model fits a\n  laptop or a small GPU, traded against tool-call accuracy.\n- **Self-hosted serving capacity.** Weight and KV-cache dtypes set how many concurrent agent sessions a\n  GPU holds.\n- **Fine-tuning SLMs.** bf16 mixed precision with fp32 master weights keeps small-model fine-tunes\n  stable and memory-efficient.\n- **Replay and regression testing.** Understanding floating-point non-determinism keeps agent replay\n  tests from flaking on harmless low-order differences.","mini_project":"**Precision ladder for a tool-calling SLM (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure what each precision step costs in agent quality.\n- **Steps:** run one small open model at bf16, 8-bit and 4-bit on 100 tool-call prompts; record memory,\n  tokens per second, JSON validity and argument accuracy; compute paired differences versus bf16 with a\n  bootstrap interval.\n- **Done when:** you have a table with intervals, a chosen precision for a 16 GB GPU and for a laptop,\n  and one paragraph explaining the trade-off with your numbers."},"exercise":"**Goal:** decide the cheapest precision a small local model can run at for your agent's tool calls.\n- **Inputs:** one open model of 0.5B to 3B parameters available in bf16 (or fp32) and at least one\n  8-bit or 4-bit variant, plus 100 tool-call prompts with expected JSON.\n- **Constraints:** same prompts, greedy decoding, same engine; record dtype and quantization method.\n- **Deliver:** a table of memory use, tokens per second, JSON validity and argument accuracy per\n  precision, with the paired difference and its interval versus the highest precision.\n- **Acceptance:** weight memory (checkpoint size or the loader's reported footprint, not total GPU\n  memory, which adds KV cache and runtime overhead) matches bytes per parameter times parameter count\n  within about 20%, or you explain the gap (scales, embeddings left unquantized);\n  your recommendation names the precision and the eval evidence; one NaN or overflow check is included.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** you build a reranker for an agent's retrieval step that scores 200 chunks with your own code,\ncomputing `exp(score / 0.05)` in fp16 on a GPU and normalizing. With some queries every chunk gets NaN and\nthe agent receives no context.\n\nSeparately, chunks whose fp32 cosine scores are 0.8313 and 0.8317 come\nout tied. Explain both failures and fix them without changing the model.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- notes that dividing by 0.05 multiplies scores by 20, so any score above about 0.55 pushes `exp` past\n  fp16's limit (x above 11.09) and creates inf, then NaN;\n- explains the ties: fp16 spacing between 0.5 and 1 is 2⁻¹¹ ≈ 0.00049, so both scores round to\n  0.83154 before the temperature is applied, and the order between them is lost;\n- fixes both with max subtraction or log-sum-exp, computed in fp32 from fp32 scores;\n- adds `isfinite` assertions and a fallback ranking so a numeric error cannot empty the agent's context;\n- adds a test with extreme scores.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Weights stored in bf16 or int4\"] --> DQ[\"Dequantize with scale\"]\n    DQ --> MM[\"Matrix multiply\"]\n    MM --> ACC[\"Accumulate in fp32\"]\n    ACC --> LG[\"Logits\"]\n    LG --> SM[\"Softmax: subtract max first\"]\n    SM --> OK[\"Valid probabilities\"]\n    LG -.->|\"naive exp in fp16\"| INF[\"inf, then NaN\"]\n    INF -.-> BAD[\"Garbage tokens or crash\"]","caption":"Follow a value through a low-precision forward pass and see where each guard sits."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between fp16 and bf16, and why did training move to bf16?","a":"- **Bit split:** both use 16 bits, but fp16 has 5 exponent and 10 mantissa bits while bf16 has 8 and 7,\n  so bf16 matches fp32's range with less precision.\n- **Why it won:** bf16 rarely overflows or underflows, so training needs no loss scaling and is more\n  stable, and modern accelerators support it natively."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent model returns empty strings for some prompts after a deployment change. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check dtype first:** confirm the loaded dtype and whether a bf16 model was cast to fp16; log inf or NaN\n  in logits for a failing prompt.\n- **Then isolate:** compare against the previous engine version and precision on the same prompts, and\n  look at maximum activation values per layer to find overflow."},{"q":"When would you not quantize a model to 4-bit?","a":"- **When the task is sensitive:** structured outputs, long tool-call arguments or arithmetic often\n  degrade first, and your paired eval shows a drop you cannot accept.\n- **When memory is not the bottleneck:** if bf16 or 8-bit fits with enough KV-cache room, the speed\n  gain may not justify the quality risk."},{"q":"How much GPU memory do the weights of a 70B model need at different precisions?","a":"- **Bytes per parameter:** about 140 GB in bf16, 70 GB in int8 and 35 GB in 4-bit, plus a little for\n  quantization scales.\n- **Then add the KV cache and activations,** which grow with context and concurrency, so the weights\n  figure is only the floor."}],"tradeoffs":["**fp16 vs bf16.** fp16 has more precision per value, but its narrow range causes overflow and needs loss scaling in training; bf16 has fp32's range but coarser rounding. bf16 wins where hardware supports it.","**4-bit vs 8-bit weights.** 4-bit halves memory again and speeds decode, but quality loss is larger and task dependent; flips toward 8-bit when tool-call accuracy drops on your eval.","**Speed vs reproducibility.** Fast fused kernels and dynamic batching change summation order, so results vary slightly between runs; deterministic modes exist but cost throughput.","**Per-tensor vs per-group scales.** Per-tensor is simplest and fastest; per-group spends a little memory on more scales to cut error from outliers."],"pitfalls":["**Casting a bf16 model to fp16.** Symptom: NaN logits, empty or repeated output on some prompts. **Diagnose** by checking the loaded dtype and logging the max absolute activation or any inf per layer on a failing prompt. Fix: load in bf16 or fp32, or use a GPU that supports bf16.","**Naive exp or log in custom code.** Symptom: NaN scores in a reranker or calibration step. **Diagnose** with `np.isfinite` checks after each step and by printing the max input to `exp`. Fix: subtract the max, use log-sum-exp, and compute in fp32.","**Trusting a quantized model's chat quality for tool use.** Symptom: more malformed JSON or wrong arguments after switching to a 4-bit checkpoint. **Diagnose** with a paired eval of tool-call validity between precisions on the same prompts. Fix: use 8-bit, a better quantization method, or constrained decoding.","**Exact-match replay tests.** Symptom: flaky tests after an engine or batch-size change. **Diagnose** by diffing outputs and finding the first divergent token at a near-tie. Fix: assert on outcomes."],"when":"- **Use when** choosing dtype or quantization for a self-hosted or local agent model, or sizing GPUs.\n- **Use when** debugging NaNs, empty generations or numerical drift in training or inference.\n- **Avoid** hand-tuning precision on hosted APIs; the provider chooses it and you evaluate outputs.\n- **Measure:** memory per configuration, tokens per second, NaN counts, and paired task-eval scores\n  per precision.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks dtypes and quantized checkpoints for agent models, keeps softmax and accumulation numerically safe in custom code, and debugs NaNs from activations and dtypes.","architect":"Sizes GPUs and edge devices from bytes per parameter, sets precision policy per deployment, and requires paired evals before any quantization change ships.","fde":"Explains to a customer why the 4-bit model that fits their laptop makes more tool-call errors, and what hardware or precision closes the gap."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to run a 13B-parameter agent model on a single 24 GB GPU that does not support\nbf16. The model was trained in bf16. What are the options, and what would you check?","a":"In bf16 or fp16 the weights alone need about 26 GB, which does not fit, and fp16 risks overflow\nfor a bf16-trained model anyway. Options: 8-bit weights at about 13 GB, leaving room for the KV cache;\n4-bit at about 6.5 GB, leaving more room but with larger quality risk; or a smaller model. Check the\nquantized model with a paired eval of tool-call validity and task success against the full-precision model\non other hardware, watch for NaN or empty outputs, and size the KV cache for the expected context."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a naive fp16 softmax of the logits [12, 10, 3] return NaN?","options":["fp16 cannot represent the number 12","exp(12) is about 162,755, beyond fp16's max of 65,504, so it becomes infinity and inf divided by inf is NaN","Softmax is undefined for more than two values","fp16 rounds every small probability to NaN"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Overflow turns exp(12) into inf, and normalizing by an infinite sum gives NaN. Subtracting the max logit first keeps every exponent at most 0 and fixes it."},{"q":"What does bf16 trade compared with fp16?","options":["More precision for less range","More range for less precision","Both more range and more precision","Neither; they are the same format"],"answer":1,"explanation":"bf16 uses 8 exponent bits like fp32, so it covers a huge range, but only 7 mantissa bits, so it rounds more coarsely; 1.001 becomes 1.0 in bf16."},{"q":"In symmetric int8 quantization, why does one large outlier weight hurt the other weights in its group?","options":["The outlier is stored in fp32 and slows the group down","Integers cannot represent negative numbers","The scale is set by the largest magnitude, so small weights get only a few coarse levels","Outliers are clipped to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The scale equals max absolute weight divided by 127, so an outlier of 2.5 makes each level about 0.0197 wide, larger than many small weights. Per-group scales or outlier handling reduce this."}]}]});
